(globalThis.TURBOPACK||(globalThis.TURBOPACK=[])).push(["object"==typeof document?document.currentScript:void 0,918845,e=>{"use strict";var t=e.i(322276);let a=[{data:[{alt_text:"Implement Queue using Stack — solution walkthrough",image_url:"/landing/infographic-sliders/slide-1.webp"},{alt_text:"Implement Min Stack Brute — step-by-step walkthrough",image_url:"/landing/infographic-sliders/slide-2.webp"},{alt_text:"Celebrity Problem — algorithm walkthrough",image_url:"/landing/infographic-sliders/slide-3.webp"}],"div-id":"tuf-image-slider-1"}],i={taglineLead:"Every problem comes with ",taglineAccent:"approach-based editorials",problemTitle:"Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array",videoThumbnailSrc:"/images/striver-thumbnail.png",revisionImageSrc:"https://static.takeuforward.org/plus/revision-images/second-highest-occurring-element-yjmgbc.webp",footer:{likes:214,dislikes:8},editorial:[{name:"Brute",revision_image:"https://static.takeuforward.org/plus/revision-images/second-highest-occurring-element-yjmgbc.webp",editorial:[{section:'<div id="editorial-video"></div>\n<h3><strong>Intuition</strong></h3>\n<p>When the array is sorted, duplicates always sit next to each other. The simplest mental model is: collect every unique value somewhere else, then write that unique sequence back into the original array from the start.</p>\n<p>A HashSet is a natural fit because it never stores the same value twice. After one pass you already know how many unique numbers exist and which values they are.</p>\n<h3><strong>Approach</strong></h3>\n<li>Create an empty HashSet.</li>\n<li>Traverse the array left to right and insert every element into the set.</li>\n<li>Let <code>K</code> be the size of the set after the pass.</li>\n<li>Copy the set elements back into the array from index <code>0</code> to <code>K - 1</code>.</li>\n<li>Return <code>K</code> as the count of unique elements.</li>\n<h3><strong>Why this works</strong></h3>\n<p>Because set insertion ignores duplicates, the final set is exactly the unique subsequence of the input. Writing it back preserves uniqueness, though relative order depends on the set implementation.</p>\n<h3>Solution</h3>\n<div id="tuf-code-editor-1"></div>\n<h3><strong>Complexity Analysis</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>Time Complexity: </strong> O(N log N) + O(N) for set operations and the copy-back pass.</p>\n<p><strong>Space Complexity: </strong> O(N) for the auxiliary HashSet in the worst case when every element is unique.</p>',examples:[],code_editor:[{...t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0],data:t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0].data.slice(0,2)}],tuf_plus_ad:[],image_slider:[],practice_link:[],video:[{asset_id:"landing-written-editorials-brute",thumbnail:"/images/striver-thumbnail.png"}],code_file_tree:[]}]},{name:"Better",revision_image:"https://static.takeuforward.org/plus/revision-images/second-highest-occurring-element-yjmgbc.webp",editorial:[{section:'<div id="editorial-video"></div>\n<h3><strong>Intuition</strong></h3>\n<p>We can avoid a full HashSet if we exploit sorted order. Duplicates form contiguous runs, so comparing each element with its immediate neighbour is enough to detect uniqueness.</p>\n<p>Still, if we allocate a second array for unique values, we pay linear extra memory. That is better than a set in practice, but not yet optimal.</p>\n<h3><strong>Approach</strong></h3>\n<li>Allocate a temporary array of the same length.</li>\n<li>Walk the input and append a value only when it differs from the previous kept value.</li>\n<li>Copy the temporary unique prefix back into the original array.</li>\n<li>Return the length of that unique prefix.</li>\n<h3><strong>Dry Run</strong></h3>\n<p>Watch how each unique value is staged into the temporary buffer before being written back.</p>\n<div id="tuf-image-slider-1"></div>\n<h3>Solution</h3>\n<div id="tuf-code-editor-1"></div>\n<h3><strong>Complexity Analysis</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>Time Complexity: </strong> O(N) for a single forward scan plus a linear copy.</p>\n<p><strong>Space Complexity: </strong> O(N) for the temporary unique buffer.</p>',examples:[],code_editor:[{...t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0],data:t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0].data.slice(0,3)}],tuf_plus_ad:[],image_slider:[...a],practice_link:[],video:[{asset_id:"landing-written-editorials-better",thumbnail:"/images/striver-thumbnail.png"}],code_file_tree:[]}]},{name:"Optimal",revision_image:"https://static.takeuforward.org/plus/revision-images/second-highest-occurring-element-yjmgbc.webp",editorial:[{section:'<div id="editorial-video"></div>\n<h3><strong>Intuition</strong></h3>\n<p>Imagine a sorted shelf where duplicate titles sit next to each other. You only need each title once, and you must keep the relative order of first occurrences.</p>\n<p>Two pointers solve this in place: one marks the last unique slot you have already committed, and the other scans ahead for the next new value. Whenever the scanner finds something new, you write it into the next unique slot and advance the write pointer.</p>\n<p>Because the array is already sorted, equal values can never reappear after a different value, so a single forward pass is enough.</p>\n<h3><strong>Approach</strong></h3>\n<li>Initialize <code>i = 0</code> as the index of the last unique element kept so far.</li>\n<li>Scan with <code>j</code> from <code>1</code> to <code>n - 1</code>.</li>\n<li>Whenever <code>nums[j] != nums[i]</code>, increment <code>i</code> and copy <code>nums[j]</code> into <code>nums[i]</code>.</li>\n<li>After the scan finishes, the unique prefix occupies indices <code>0 … i</code>.</li>\n<li>Return <code>i + 1</code> as the count of unique elements.</li>\n<h3><strong>Dry Run</strong></h3>\n<p>Follow the two pointers across a sample array. The image viewer below highlights each write into the unique prefix.</p>\n<div id="tuf-image-slider-1"></div>\n<h3>Solution</h3>\n<p>The same algorithm is shown across languages so you can map the pointer logic to the syntax you use in interviews.</p>\n<div id="tuf-code-editor-1"></div>\n<h3><strong>Multi-file walkthrough</strong></h3>\n<p>Larger production problems often split controllers, services, and domain helpers. The file tree mirrors that layout so you can jump between related symbols the way you would in a real codebase review.</p>\n<div id="tuf-code-file-tree-1"></div>\n<h3><strong>Complexity Analysis</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>Time Complexity: </strong> O(N) — each index is visited at most once.</p>\n<p><strong>Space Complexity: </strong> O(1) — only a constant number of pointers are used; the array is updated in place.</p>\n<h3><strong>Edge cases</strong></h3>\n<li>Empty input → return <code>0</code>.</li>\n<li>All elements identical → return <code>1</code>.</li>\n<li>Already unique sorted array → return <code>n</code> with no extra writes beyond the scan.</li>',examples:[],code_editor:[{...t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0],data:t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_EDITOR[0].data}],tuf_plus_ad:[],image_slider:[...a],practice_link:[],video:[{asset_id:"landing-written-editorials",thumbnail:"/images/striver-thumbnail.png"}],code_file_tree:[...t.SOLUTION_PANEL_CODE_FILE_TREE]}]}]};a[0].data;let o={taglineLead:"One shareable portfolio for",taglineAccent:"everything you build",brief:{fullName:"Mukti Markam",username:"mukti.unfiltered",statusLabel:"Professional",headline:"SDE-2 @ Razorpay",location:"Bengaluru, India",profileViews:"5,200 profile views",publicUrl:"takeuforward.org/profile/muks.u...",avatarImageUrl:"/landing/profile-preview/avatar.webp",bannerImageUrl:"/landing/profile-preview/banner.webp"},about:"Backend engineer with 4 years of experience building payment infrastructure at scale. I care about clean system design, mentoring juniors, and writing about distributed systems. Currently exploring event-driven architectures and preparing for staff-level interviews.",projects:[{id:"payment-reconciliation",title:"Payment Reconciliation Engine",access:"Confidential",description:"Internal engine reconciling 40M+ daily transactions across ledgers with sub-minute latency.",tags:["Go","Kafka","PostgreSQL"],imageUrl:"/landing/profile-preview/project-payment-reconciliation.webp"},{id:"go-ratelimit",title:"go-ratelimit",access:"GitHub",description:"Token-bucket rate limiter library with Redis backends for multi-instance deployments.",tags:["Go","Redis","OSS"],imageUrl:"/landing/profile-preview/project-go-ratelimit.webp"},{id:"dsa-visualizer",title:"DSA Visualizer",access:null,description:"Interactive graph & DP algorithm visualizer used by 30k+ students monthly.",tags:["TypeScript","React"],imageUrl:"/landing/profile-preview/project-dsa-visualizer.webp"}],consistency:{platformsConnected:5,platformTabs:["All","TUF","LeetCode","CodeChef","Codeforces"],rangeLabel:"12 Months",stats:[{label:"Contributions",value:"1,284"},{label:"Active days",value:"211"},{label:"Best streak",value:"124"},{label:"Contests",value:"36"}],heatmapLevels:[0,1,0,2,1,0,3,2,0,1,4,2,1,0,3,1,2,0,1,3,4,2,1,0,2,3,1,4,2,0,1,2,3,1,0,4,2,1,3,0,1,2,4,3,1,0,2,1,3,4,2,1,0,3,2,1,4,0,2,3,1,2,0,4,1,3,2,0,1,4,3,2,1,0,3,2,4,1,0,2,3,1,4,2,0,1,3,2,4,1,0,2,3,1,2,4,0,1,3,2,1,0,4,2,3,1,0,2,1,4,3,0,2,1,3,4,2,1,0,3]},achievements:{count:9,titles:[{id:"1",label:"Explorer"},{id:"2",label:"Pathfinder"}],trophies:[{id:"consistency-1",label:"Consistency I"},{id:"consistency-2",label:"Consistency II"},{id:"consistency-3",label:"Consistency III"},{id:"practice-1",label:"Practice I"},{id:"practice-2",label:"Practice II"},{id:"learning-1",label:"Learning I"},{id:"learning-2",label:"Learning II"}]},dsaProgress:{platforms:["TUF","LeetCode"],byPlatform:{TUF:{platform:"TUF",solved:567,total:1343,categories:[{label:"Basic",solved:197,total:334},{label:"Core",solved:197,total:457},{label:"Pro",solved:197,total:253}],topics:[{label:"Arrays",solved:400,total:480},{label:"Dynamic Programming",solved:387,total:460},{label:"String",solved:302,total:380},{label:"Hashmap and Set",solved:283,total:340},{label:"Sorting",solved:194,total:250},{label:"Greedy algorithm",solved: